Beginning a New Wave Crisis
“France and the rest of Europe at the present time are in the spotlight of investors.
A statistical vacillation swept over Europe on Tuesday.
Separate countries unveiled data on GDP in the third quarter.
Compared with the previous quarter, the Czech economy was stagnation in a state of stagnation, Germany and France are showing growth.
And yet the debt market today breaks records.
Investors are already looking not only in the direction of Italy; now they are focused on Spain, France, Austria and other countries.
The statistical offices of the countries of the eurozone in Germany, the Czech Republic and some other countries unveloped their preliminary estimates of economic development in the third quarter.
Germany's GDP increased by 0.5%; France - by 0.4%.
Both countries also adjusted the data for the second quarter, in which Germany went better than expected (an increase of 0.3% instead of an initial 0.1%). France reported a slight decrease, by 0.1% (initial information implied stagnation).
In the third quarter of this year the Czech economy showed a preliminary increase of 1.5%.
However, in comparison with the previous quarter GDP was in a state of stagnation. According to the Czech statistical office, this confirmed the trend of a gradual slowdown in economic growth.
The results turned out to be worse, than analysts predicted.
Key Events:
In the third quarter of this year the Czech economy showed a preliminary increase of 1.5%; however, it was in a state of stagnation compared to the previous quarter.
Based on spectrength and seasonal data, in the third quarter, Germany’s economy grew by 0.5%.
From July to September, the economy of both the eurozone and the European Union showed an increase compared with the previous quarter by 0.2%, which coincides with analysts' forecasts.
The economy of Greece showed a preliminary decrease of 5.2%.
The yield of its 10-year-old bonds is 28.45%.
Yields of both Italian and Spanish bonds are approaching a critical level.
Even Austria, the Netherlands, France, and Belgium, etc. are beginning to experience difficulties.
Despite the fact that the situation in the region is deteriorating, the profitability of Czech 1-year bonds remains about 4%.
In Germany, it is thwarted that the ECB is the last creditor who can save the situation.
