The dynamics of travel prices since 1989
Until 1989, only five state travel agencies could work in former Czechoslovakia: Čedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekre and Autoturist.
Since traveling to the West was severely confined, travel agencies at that time mainly sold tours to the countries of the socialist bloc.
Thus, on vacation Czech tourists went to the German Democratic Republic; the Soviet Union (Yochi, Crimea on the Black Sea); Bulgaria; Romania (both countries on the Black Sea); and Hungary (Balaton).
The only exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when we had the opportunity to travel to Yugoslavia. The Yugoslav regime then turned to the West, which entailed significant restrictions on the travel of Czech tourists to Yugoslavia.
The great paradox of the socialist period was the fact that citizens of the socialist bloc (i.e. Czechoslovakia) did not allow individual visits to socialist countries.
If a Czechoslovakian citizen wanted to go by himself, for example, to the Soviet Union or Poland, it was necessary an invitation letter from this country; an individual journey in another way was not possible.
At that time, travel agencies offered trips to the West, but it was necessary either to stand queue all the night, with a sleeping bag, or have “good friends” in the agency.
And that wasn't everything.
A foreign currency resolution was necessary for trips to the West. It wasn't given politically unreliable people who weren't allowed to travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not freely convertible; it was not possible to obtain cash (the Western currency) for a trip to the West.
Most often, the Czechs spent their holidays at home, which led to the appearance of a large number of cottages and summer houses, this habit is preserved to this day.
After 1989, tourism in the Czech Republic thus experienced a real revolution.
At the same time, until 1989 vacation in the GDR or Bulgaria was the culmination of our travels, last year the Czechs spent 4.5 million holidays in countries around the country around the world.
The Great Paradox, which we are no longer aware, that until 1989 we were not able to freely visit Horvail at the present time, since it was part of the former Yugoslavia.
At the very beginning of 1990, the need to obtain a visa to the neighboring countries of Western Europe was abolished. This gave rise to a huge wave of short trips, mainly to Vienna and Germany.
Czechoslovaks could not be saturated with trips to the West. Everyone wanted to go there at least for a moment.
For example, in Paris, Czech tourists slept in tanning parks, since their income at that time was not enough for a hotel or boarding room.
A typical feature of Czech tourism at that time there was a desire to bring your own food (canned food, salami, etc.) to save money.
Another important point is the introduction of a freely convertible croner. decades later, our tourists are finally able to freely buy Western currency.
Our standard of living and the average income grew, which around 1997 led to another important circumstance.
Since 1997, the increased demand has allowed travel agencies to prepare regular flights carried out by charter airlines compared to the past, flights have now become incredibly cheap thanks to the opportunity to order wholesale discounts.
By the end of the 1990's, the guys didn't need a visa to visit almost all the developed world countries, except the United States. This made our travels even easier.
The next significant event in the history of voyages after the revolution took place in the year 2000. Great demand led to the historically first direct charter flight from the Czech Republic to an exotic country, Thailand.
Since then, direct charter flights are delivered by Czech tourists to warm exotic countries. This in the winter, flights from the Czech Republic will be sent to twelve exotic destinations.
At the moment, about 100 thousand Chets visit exotic countries every year!
And another important development of this period, symbolizing our development after the revolution, took place in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the United States abolished the need to obtain a visa for Czech tourists. Our country thus forever joined the world's most well-developed countries.
For the first time in the history of 2008, the Czechs spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%); and for the first time in history they spent more than 15 thousand kroner per person during their vacation abroad, including their spending at its destination.
The general meeting should elect the president, the threat of the path returns
For about another five months, Czech football did not have a president. There is a risk that the successor of Ivan Gashek, the head of the Czech Football Association (FAČR) will not be named even after another extraordinary general meeting, which will be held in Nimbürk on Tuesday.
There is still no agreement on which rules of the elections to follow.
Without this, voting for one of the three candidates will not begin.
Yindrikh Reichl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomasz Pazlik craze to lead Czech football.
Hasek resigned from his post in June. Leaving, he urged his colleagues to unite and to find a strong personality who would lead football in the future.
Nevertheless, the era of Gashek again plundered football in strife between the two chambers of the association, which can not choose a single candidate.
This became quite obvious on September 16, when it was necessary to elect a new president.
Delegates of the general assembly in vain came to the National House in Prague in Prague, Smikhov, because the negotiations remained blocked, not only an election procedure.
There has been no agreement on someone who has the right to vote at a general meeting - only lawful representatives or also officials who have been empowered.
At this point has since been partially resolved thanks to the recommendation of the Institute of the State and the Law, according to which representation based on the powers confiscations is not possible.
Controversy about the rules of the elections, however, is ongoing.
The Executive Council also did not help too much, although in its meetings, both regular and extraordinary, it is mainly occupied by this issue.
Ideas about the methods of voting changed several times, but the key issue remains the same.
The question is as follows - how to go to round 3 and to avoid the path?
“The dispute is whether to follow established norms or to find another model,” said one of the vice-presidents of FAČAR Dusan Svoboda, who introduces professional football in the management of an association.
Based on the model stipulated by the charter, the winner of round 3 is the candidate who has received great support from the chamber from which he acts as a candidate.
Moravia agrees to a compromising winner of an election candidate with the largest number of voters in both houses.
the Czech side insists on the condition that in the total percentage, the winner must receive at least two-thirds of the vote.
"Necessarily, the two previous rounds at that time would have lost their meaning; this would completely reject the concept of the two chambers," Svoboda agrees with Moravia's argument. He says that it would be better not to change the rules and vote in accordance with the existing rules.
Susceptibly, Tomas Pazlik agrees with his opinion, although according to the election rules, determined by the charter, arrange Pelta more than other candidates.
“I’d like the elections to take place as soon as possible.”
"I want to avoid all sorts of representative nonsense, as at the previous general meeting," says owner Victoria Pilsen, who is the candidate for the for the first time; and in his statements he largely contradicted Peleta.
Pelta remains an optimist.
He believes that the new president will become known in Nimburka; he also hopes that in the future the candidates will cooperate constructively.
"All emotional ferment will calm down after today's expected exit of the national team for the European Championship."
"Football will again be on a happily wave, people will be more willing to cooperate," the representative of Jablonets says. Of the three candidates, he has the most experience in football management at both the professional and and regional levels.
Reichle is more restrained.
"I look forward to an agreement, but the situation is very ambiguous."
"This is not between representatives of Bohemia and Moravia, but there is an attempt to intentionally adapt rules."
"This may cause another disruption of the general meeting."
“I’m hoping that common sense will win,” Reichl said.
There is not a lot of time left for the parties to reach an agreement.
According to some reports, individual negotiations took place directly in Montenegrin; but who knows whether football unity will be achieved before the match.
An additional flight, which had to deliver, in addition to the other vice-presidents of Dailor Kučeru and Reichla, did not fly from Prague in the morning due to a technical malnutrition. We were looking for an alternative in order to bring part of the executive committee to the place of the replay.
In the end, the meeting at the level of the Minister, which is expected on Wednesday, can be decisive.
The Minister of education, youth and sports, Josef Dobesh, I would like the adversarial parties as part of the strongest sports association in the country to come to an agreement.
There are a lot at stake: governmental subsidies; the creditworthiness of football; and the planned ČSTV general meeting with a new directorate of Czech sports after the financial problems of Sasky.
Pazlik enters the presidential wrestling, because he does not see a suitable candidate
If at an extraordinary general meeting, the head of the players Pilsen, Tomasz Pazlik, would have seen a strong candidate who would have complied with all of his requirements for the President of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR), he wouldn’t even apply for a high post.
"If there was a worthy candidate, I'd be fighting for him and I didn't participate in the fight."
“But such a person has not yet been found,” Pazlik ČTK said.
Pazlick’s motivity to enter the fight against Miroslav Pelta and Yinjich Reich is to try to harass Pelta, an alleged electoral favorite, to join the FAČR.
Moreover, Pazlik emphasizes that in the event of the victory, he will remain president only until 2013, when the next general meeting is planned.
