Not more than eleven per cent believe that investments that reliance on the price of food are acceptable for Douche Bank.
Many banking customers would also take concrete steps if they found that their bank is involved in such trading. According to a study, every second would withdraw deposits, which ultimately lead to specutization with goods.
43% would recommend leaving the bank, and 49 percent said that they will think about closing an invoice and switching to another bank.
Category th 21 th culling in Vienna
Pavilion in a new place
Ingeborg Winsowski
An architectural history with a rise, falls and a happy ending. The pavilion of Austria for the World's E-Exhibition of 1958 in Brussels conquered prizes, became a museum in Vienna, emptied and was announcing.
Now it is redisclosed as an exhibition center.
The pavilion of Austria, Designed by Karl Schwanzer for the 1958 World's Exhibition in Brussels, is a well-known exemplary example of modern post-war architecture with its measile shapes, glass halls, who bravely expressed belief in technological progress.
Schwanzera Pavilion conquered the Architecture Grand Prix in 1958 for his easy-floating design made of made of steel and glass.
The building is considered a Milestone in Modern Architecture.
This made the architect so well-known that he also built the Austrian pavilion at the next World's Fair.
In the end, he became famous all over all over the world in 1973 for his legendary four-villiand turquo BMW and its sister - a bowl building.
Confused as a building for temporary exhibitions has been relocated to the Swiss garden near the Southern Station of Vienna, transformed and opened as the Museum of the 20 th century in 1962 - and is often referred to as "House of the 20th century" for this reason.
Be that as it may, the building was not suitable for the museum for technical and spatial reasons - there were no walls on which you could hung works of art and poor air conditioning.
However, however, it continued to be used until the modern art in Vienna received a new home.
The house has been emptied since 2001, and its condition was getting worse and getting worse.
Architect Adolf Krishanitz has now rehabilitated the pavilion, which was titled "House of the 21 th century" and will be used as a museum of modern art.
This museum is a branch of the Belvedere Museum, which will open the first exhibition on Tuesday evening - in all senses a joyful event.
Agnès Husslein-Arco, who became the new director of the Austrian Belweder Gallery in 2007, contributed to a new beginning.
Husslein-Arco, a graduate of the Faculty of History of the Arts with a gorgeous skater in her youth who worked in Sotheby's, the Guggenheim Museum and eventually became the founder and director of the Museum of Moderation in Salzburg, persuaded policy makers to continue the refurbishment, attracted public funds and private donations and decided on how the building will be used.
The new museum was supposed to accommodate a permanent exhibition of the Frytiz Votruba Foundation, a café and a bookstore along with modern art. An office building should have also been to be built.
Strict Control & Restricted Budget
Krishanitz at that time was an ideal candidacy to carry out the project.
He was well-knowided, of course due to the restoration and enlargement of the Viennese Secession, Josef Olbrich, and won the competition, but he also studied at Schwanzer at the University of Viennese technology, and the pavilion was for him “an extremely important place”, where he visited exhibitions “almost every week”.
He knew the original design and knew the tasks that stood in front of the building, a building that had already lost part of its greatness and ease when it was moved to Vienna and transformed.
Troubled problems that arose during the last perestroika were much more serious. The building was an "energy absorber", says Krishanitz.
The window profile on the glass faucet was replaced, reinforced with fiberglass-strengthened safety glass was inserted, and the glass roof has been replaced by high-strength safe glass.
The steel ferrors of the thermal bridge were amplified or replaced, and asbestos was removed.
The two lower-floor floors have created a space for a new use of the building, a swing well between the building and the street provides lighting - almost a new building, but a still graceful Schwanzer Pavilion.
The original external doors have been preserved, and the building has been painted in an old red-brown corrosion protection.
The hall of the cinema is the same as it used to be.
All of this with the strict control of the museum management and with a limited budget.
"We often had to find a material that was good, cheap, and would be approved by the museum management," Krishanitz says, pointing to an uneven outstring in the basement.
The building remained flexible and easy, regardless of limitations, problems and transformation.
Krishanitz recently remarked the pavilion "from Brussel" was rude - a typical "drop in the city".
Such an architecture “always is not in its place”, and nevertheless wants to “stimulate real awareness”.
The house of the 21 th century is in a very correct place, because when planning the city there was an unexpected luck. A completely restructured "quarter of the main station" with offices and residential real estate grows around the Swiss garden.
A second-hapneous event may take place. The house of the 21-th century can be complemented by Krishanitz's second building - a collector of Francesca von Gabsburg has acquired the Temporary Exhibition Hall of Berlin and is reported to be in negotiations with the city about its location next door to the Swiss Garden.
