Bank Scandal on Lake Zürikh
The majority of the leading risky policies of European banks are located not in France or in Italy, but in Switzerland.
UBS almost went bankrupt during the financial crisis and has been invariably at odds with one or the other crisis.
Now to return to the right course of the German should.
Alex Weber didn't realize he'd lost that role.
In July, after his spectacular resignation from the post of head of the Federal Bank, it was announced that in 2013 he would begin to work in an equally effective post - the Chairman of the Board of Directors of the largest Swiss bank UBS; an unexpected and lucrative career move.
Alex Weber was clearly enthusiastic, practising Switzerland for its beauties, UBS for his newfound strength and made some friendly remarks about her future colleagues.
“UBS managed to change for the better.”
“I look forward to working with Caspar Philliger and Oswald Grubel.”
But already in September it became clear that everything will be completely different.
UBS trader in London surpassed the bank’s security system and lost about 1.7 billion euros.
UBS CEO Oswald Grubel - a banker who is known not only in Switzerland - had to leave.
Since then, the chairman of UBS is considered to be patient, and everyone wants to know: when will Alex Weber finally take office?
Webér has been advising the bank since February 2012.
And, as the bank stated on Tuesday, the post of Chairman of the Board it will take place as soon as in May, and not in 2013.
It won’t be easy for Alex Weber.
He is a professor of economics and a specialist of the Central Bank, not a traditional banker, and he will undoubtedly need time to adapt.
Suddenly he was responsible for the strategy of the bank, which once again proved that he has long earned the title of “largest banking scandal in Europe”.
UBS, on the one hand, is very large - until recently he managed the largest assets in the world - and, on the other - for years it does not get out of the scandals for years.
It seems that the proud Swiss bank does not spare anything.
It all started during the Financial Crisis.
Until 2007, UBS had an excellent recompiliation.
The bank combines an image of a stealthy and stable, but at the same time agrious asset manager with the ambitious goals and the desire to become the largest player in investment banking.
As a Result in 2007, UBS managed 1.6 billion, thus managing more wealth of customers than any other bank in the world.
and as an investment bank entered the league of the largest banks in the world.
This made an impression.
Hans Geiger, a former banker and a professor of banking who has long taught at the Zurich Institute of Banking, explains: “I’ve always quoted UBS as an example of a good bank.”
"Knowing what we're aware right now, you might ask how I could have been so mistaken."
He's really really wrong.
Only in 2007 it became clear that something is going wrong at UBS at all.
In the middle of the middle of the year, Executive Director Peter Wuffley - who was considered an eminent intellectual and to the same extent a brilliant banker - unexpectedly retired from his post.
A few months later, we were able to guess why he left.
The investment division of the bank tried to play on substandard securities and was forced to write off billions.
You want to believe you don't want, but UBS reported an increase of $13 billion of Swiss francs to withstand the crisis.
The bank was indeed able to find investors – but investors whose services many bankers would have politely unleashed – one very opaque Asian sovereign wealth fund from Singapore and an Arab sovereign fund, hidden so much that even its name has not been communicated.
It was only the first hit.
In April 2008, everything became much worse.
The bank had to announcing a new billion-dollar losses, and Marcel Ospel’s second-largest-ranked chairman of the board of directors in Switzerland in 2006 resigned.
In October 2008, there was a precarriage. Due to the lack of investment during the Financial Crissis, UBS found itself in the threat of collapsing, and the state had to rescue it.
The National Bank repurchased UBS toxic merchandise securities for an undiscellaneccable number of billions, and the Swiss Confederacy has paid six billion Swiss francs for a convertible bond to become a co-owner of a bank.
The whole of Switzerland was astonished: the country realized the dramatic consequences that huge banks settled in a small country.
In the end, UBS's final balance in 2007 was five times larger than the entire production of Switzerland (see graph).
“We won’t be able to save UBS for a second time,” says banking analyst Geiger.
The second disaster occurred shortly after the first.
A few months later, UBS capitulated in a tax dispute with the U.S. and said that it was ready to transfer data from wealthy customers, who may have evaded taxes with the help of the bank.
UBS also agreed to pay $7,780 million, consisting of reimbursement of undentitious business proceeds plus fines.
UBS thus more or less recognized that it was a tax evasion tool.
At the same time, UBS has lost what has always been its advantage in the struggle for wealthy clients - a veil of secrecy.
After this scandal, Swiss bank secrequity was no longer the same as it used.
The Executive Director and the chairman of the board of directors retired, and
Began the expiration of customers' money.
For several months, the bank has lost confidence in both main areas of its activities - in investing due to unsuccessful specutions and in the management of assets as a result of helping and encouraging tax evasion.
And only everything calmed down and it began to seem that UBS managed to break the situation, how this catastrophe happened with the scammer-trader. A single trader lowered 1.7 billion euros, before the bank noticed that something is wrong.
One thing is clear - the management of risks in the investment department does not yet work.
“Adoboli’s Incident has clearly shown that risk control has not changed enough,” says Martin Janssen, professor of economic and finance at the Swiss Institute of Banking at Zuyrich University.
The chief financial officer of one of the largest German banks also has no doubts: "The problem of UBS is that he does not have a tool to control the risks."
Caution for a long time made Swiss bankers custodians custodians of the largest assets in the world - not an unnecessary risk, but security for the money, regardless of whether the world has collapsed outside the alpine republic or hasn't.
The boxes of the Swiss banks have become a symbol of unshakable requisition, as Lindt and Sprüngli symbolize a refined taste in chocolate.
The UBS headquarters in Zurich still corresponds to the image of a dignified Swiss private bank - only a little more.
Its location - in itself a classic.
The main office of the bank is located in Zurich on Bangoffstrasse, which leads from the main railway station directly to Zurich Lake, encircled by snow caps of the Swiss mountains.
Buildings here are gorgeous, and rental rates for stores are one of the tallest in Europe.
Among the traditional Swiss stores such as Confisrie Sprüngli here you can find such jewelry firms as Tiffany's.
Swiss jaiges were located here for hundreds of years - from the National Bank to Crudhi Suisse Palace and luxury private banks, such as Julius Baer.
Severe door leads to UBS lobby.
Aqularous walls are large leather sofas. Near the emblem of the jar of a large indicator "Safe".
UBS knows what its customers need - reliability.
But the two largest Swiss banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, at some point renounced the Swiss concepts of banking - or, according to the head of one of the small Swiss banks, betrayed them.
He wouldn’t want to see his name in the newspaper – like all Swiss bankers, he also cooperates with the two largest banks.
He believes UBS has become too large - in particular, in investment banking, where the Anglo-Saxons are very active.
In his opinion, the Swiss have nothing to add here.
“And if there’s anything, then they have long adapted to the Anglo-Saxon customs.”
Indeed, the Anglo-Saxon investment banking changed UBS, brought him the first large profits - and then a fiasco. No other financial institution of Europe has lost so much money in the jungle of substandard assets, like the largest Swiss bank.
Catastrophe became evident on October 15, 2008.
“On that Wednesday the ruling coalition of a small country with a population of seven million people took the largest rescue operation in the history of the country,” Lukas Hesssig writes in his book The Collapse of UBS.
Politicians approximated the allocation of 68 billion Swiss francs (about 45 billion euros) to salute the bank from bankrupt.
How'd you get to that?
If you ask the Swiss cans, they will say that the problem is mainly UBS.
Two of the three largest Swiss banks merged into one large group in June 1998.
A bank was planted, which already at the time of the merging was too large for small Switzerland - and the bank, which continued to grow.
This was facilitated by the fact that the two banks that formed UBS, complemented each other. Alone was a rich but power bank specializing in Zurich in the management of assets; the other was not so rich but much more internationalized.
Inter-nationalized investment bankers abruptly gained access to a large amount of money, which they could use to twist the almighty Wheel.
Two names are associated with UBS growth: Peter Woolfley, chief executive of UBS from 2001 to 2007, and Marcel Ospel, chairman.
“I’ve always believed that Wollffli and Ospel are a dream team,” says banking analyst Hans Geiger.
Wulffley, an eminent anticulent, humble and deeply understood models and statistics, and Ospel with his inner institute, who have gone all the way, starting with an indiner at the bank."
But Ospel eventually relied on Wulffley.
And Vulffley could make a critical mistake. "He decided his models were a reality," Geiger said.
“It’s tragic.”
Possible it is tragic.
Pority precedes defeat.
UBS servicemen are well-reminisceos of boardmates at which Woolffley always repeated the same motivate Goldman Sachs.
The U.S. bank was the world's most powerful investment bank.
At that time, he headed the “tournament table”, where the achievements of the banks were made.
UBS wanted to get at the very top of this table.
He was able to do part of that path.
UBS was the largest player in trading in currency and government securities.
Mandatory management consultants, however, pointed to a weak point. According to the parameter, which in banking terminology is called "permanent income", UBS was far behind the largest banks.
UBS had failures in U.S. Mortgage products.
To plug the hole, UBS got into an an adventur that almost cost him life.
Striving for growth, the bank itself and the hedge-based fund invested in American sub-standard securities.
This developed into a large mechanism, which combined securities based on American mortgage loans and sold them further - continuing to bear the risks themselves.
Even when other jailes have long been looking for a way to get out of this process, UBS didn't stop.
"UBS bought until the summer of 2007, when the American real estate market was already on fire", - says banking analyst Geiger.
The higher the growth, the deeper the fall.
One manager after another had to go away as it was more difficult to put the bank in order than at first seemed.
posts in UBS turned into a children's game with chairs and until this day remains unreliable.
Before anyone who heads this huge bank, there is an almost impossible task.
He must find a strategy for the bank, which turned its back on all existing business models.
Geiger's banking analyst already has an idea - to abandon investment banking, to focus on the management of the assets and forget about their Swiss roots.
The bank, however (still) is not ready to go so far.
Nevertheless, it is obvious that investment activity needs to be significantly squeezed.
The new head of the bank Sergio Ermotti plans to present his future strategy on Tuesday.
The 51-year-already Italo-Switzercair was actually considered only as an interim solution, when the bank unexpectedly broke up with its predecessor Grubel.
Over the past time, he managed to show the character and now, according to the Zurich bank on Tuesday, he heads UBS on a permanent basis.
Ernotti plans to return the investment bank to the state in the middle of the 90-ies.
It reduces 3500 jobs.
It won’t be enough.
UBS needs the concept of development.
What is UBS?
How is he planning to make money?
These questions probably ask Axel Weber.
Publicly, he still only said that he and his wife are looking for an apartment in Zurich.
In the next year.
