SAP Expects to Double the Number of Employees in China
The largest software maker in Europe, SAP, plans to invest billions and almost doubt the number of its workers in China in the next few years.
Dax-based company said on Thursday it was going to invest about two billion dollars (about €1.5 billion) by 2015.
"We will create more opportunities for research and development and hire thousands of people," Bill McDermott, one of SAP's executive directors, said in a statement.
States out of 2,500 should grow to 4500.
The leader of the global industrial software market plans to redouble the number of its offices from five to ten or eleven.
SAP currently has offices in Beijing, Shanghai, Guanzhou, Chengdu and Dalana.
In this country, experiencing an economic boom, there are about 4000 SAP customers from more than 176 000 around the world.
China’s builder calculation is an important emplacement of the 2015 strategy of this company, which is listed in Dax.
SAP plans to increase the turnover of up to 20 billion euros by 2015.
Last year, Waldorf company earned approximately 12.5 billion euros.
A software provider with 55 000 employees around the world looks better than ever.
SAP has been present in China for 20 years.
"Now we want to change the scale of our operations," McDermotte said.
“Our goal is to achieve sustainable growth, relying on informatization in China.”
