Translated with the Swedish Peter Grave, the prose of Mr. Englund is fugitive but discreet, beautifully suitable for his humanistic task.
In dozens of small scenes, it shows how the war “liberated completely uncontrollable forces: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred.”
“People begin to ask why their leaders are forcing them to fight.
The best books about World War I were often indistinguished, like Paul Fassel’s “Great War and Modern Memory”, or such romances such as Erich-Marie Remarque’s “On the Western Front Without Change”, rather than exhaustive historical works.
