The walls have eyes: As researchers are studying you on Facebook
Before becoming the new face of right-wing extremism in Europe, Anders Bering Breivik was an ordinary guy exploding their anti-immigration views on the Internet.
Breuwyick, who confessed to a mass murder in Norway in July that claimed the lives of 77 people, appeared on his first open court hearing.
Although Breivik could have acted alone, he wasn’t alone on the network: he spent a lot of time preparing for an attack at his computer, communicating with the millions of nationalists who support right-wing groups on social media.
After the summer tragedies, the explorers wanted to learn more about these people.
But how to find them?
Easily - you just need to go to Facebook.
“We’ve figured it doesn’t have a hard time,” said Jamie Bartlet, the lead author of the recently published report on European cyber-populism of the British DEMOS Research Center.
The stock of personal data on Facebook is so great, Bartlett says that the researchers could easily use an advertising tool of the site in order to highlight the necessary demographic group with a scientific andteatibility - which marketers have been doing for several years.
Bartlet's team found half a million fans of right-wing bands all over Europe and then slipped them an advertisement, but instead of displaying a new group or a dietary product, advertising offered the user a survey in which there were questions about their level of education, attitude to violence and optimism about their own future.
Using Facebook, Bartlet and his collaborators were able to interview more than 10,000 participants of 14 far-right batches in 11 European countries - not even leaving the office.
“It’s a completely new way of doing research,” says Bartlett.
Some results are not so astonishing: the online supporters of right-wing groups are most often young men who are dissatisfied with immigration.
“Those who combine their online activity with off-activity are more sensible, more democratic, and less aggressive than those who only hide behind the monitor.”
DEMOS is just one example of how Facebook is becoming a new popular tool in the hands of scientists.
Research centers, medical researchers and political analysts use the site to study everything in the world - from issues of health to social trends, which are expressed in “Likes”, posts on the wall and status renovations.
With More Than 800 Million Active Users Adding An Average Of Three Wreverhee Wreveruges Per Day, Facebook’s “super-relevant information” causes a research boom by an increasing number of scientific articles with a site title by an estimated target by an estimated last five years.
For some researchers, the beauty of Facebook is that it provides an opportunity to be able to study people who can’t be approached on the street with a notebook.
"Before, if you wanted to interview people from the British National Party, it would be very difficult to identify them."
"You'd have to join the party, and they'd never accept you," Bartlet said.
"Facebook excludes all of this - you turn to them directly."
Others say that with the help of this site it would be possible to Identify and Explain Social Health Issues.
The latest study by Dr. Meghan Moreno of Wisconsin University, Maddison, and her colleagues found that the students who discussed their drunken feats on Facebook were significantly more at risk of an alcoholic than the students who remained silent about it.
Moreno assumes that the peers of the students, for example, their dormitory mentors, could monitor the site and intervene to help a student who too often renovates drunken statuses.
“You can’t solve the problem without revealing it,” Dmitri Christakis, co-author of Moreno, director of the Center for Child Health, Behavioral and Development at the Siethla Children's Research Institute, said.
“We have found a way to Identify at-to-Hifficisan children who wouldn’t otherwise be found.”
Facebook also continually retrieve all sorts of data about its users.
The site's data processing unit processed the statistics of relations status and St. Valentine's day, voter turnout in the middle of 2010, as well as the National Happiness Index (mainly using the information they only have access - the champions of cyberfree for a long time insisted that the site should make its vast collection of data publicly available for research).
But Facebook users are aware that the site is watching them, whether they like it or not - in exchange for the opportunity to play Scrubble with a friend located thousands of miles from you, the site can process your personal data.
But what to do when Facebook users become participants in a study they don’t even know about?
While the researchers have strictly defined off-stuning standards, the online data is publicly available.
“I don’t think a lot of users at least wonder what a researcher can observe their profiles,” says Neil Selvene, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Laboratory, about a study that he conducted in 2006 using public pages of students on Facebook.
“As far as they imagined, it wasn’t between them and their friends.”
Then the question arises about the methodology.
There’s not even an off-line guarantee that the object of the study is extremely honest.
It’s not easy to find out on Facebook how much of the information in the profile and on the user’s page is true.
“What you’re saying on Facebook and what you’re doing outside of Facebook are two completely different things,” Selwin says.
That’s why, in spite of his years of work in the field of technological research, Selwin thinks that a notebook and a pen are still the best research tools you can use.
“Nothing can replace access to the real world and communication with real people,” he says.
It is assumed that social research is about the social, and most of the social is still happening offline.
