Climatic Problems Squeeze Economies
During Mario Lópeza’s first rule, Unemployment and Illegal labour markets rose in Sinaloa, and foreign funding declined and the economy was weakened.
The year 2011, which will go into the economic history of Singola as associated with the first year of Mario López Waldés, also referred to the year of change, which gave rise to great hopes for the development of the economy but, given the unfavourable circulatory conditions at the beginning of the year, there was no growth, but there was a decrease in the main indicators of economic development.
February low temperatures not only led to the losses of millions for the agricultural sector, but they have confined economic growth, causing a reduction in economic activism by 3.6 per cent in the first half of the year, mainly due to a historic decline of 31.16 per cent in agriculture, which has an effect on the dynamics of other sectors of the economy.
According to a report on areas Affected by frost provided by the Mexican Council for Sustainable Rural Development, 835,582 hectectas have been affected, of which 78.03 per cent have been affected in full and the remaining 21.07 per cent have been partially affected by crops, maize, maize and chickpeas.
The decline in economic activity, until November, a constant increase in unemployment in the state, according to the National Institute of Statistics and Geography, an accelerated increase in the ungiveness during the first three quarters was an accelerated increase in the unemployment rate, from January to March 55,053 residents of Sinalo were unemplounced, with a share of 4.53 percent of an economically active population, in the second quarter the share has risen to 5.28 percent, and from July to September it continued to grow to 6.19 percent, a share.
Decreased Liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa within a year is discussed by many factors, for economist Gerardo López Cervantes, director of the Faculty of Economics and Social Sciences of the Autonomous University (UAS), the increase in unemployment can be explained indirectly by actions that were undertaken in public policy.
“What we’re seeing right now is an increase in unemployment that is linked to a number of factors, including the loss of liquidity in the market to limit the resources imposed by a law that has been enacted in Congress to combat money laundering, as people who illegally own dollars are difficult to work with them, to invest and use them in something,” he said.
Refused immigration
López Cervantes said that there is also a demographic factor in an increase in unemployment.
“The number of people who are leaving the state looking for a job abroad has been confined, primarily due to difficulties with the in-entry, and in the second - since, once, they already face very many difficulties when looking for a job, so that a group of people remains and demands jobs here, in the state, as a result, the unemployment rate is growing," he said.
Violation of Formal Claim
Sinaloa’s labor market analysis is getting worse if we pay attention a large number of violations that predominate in employment, according to INEGI, 60.38 per cent of the active population of the state works without being mentioned as employees in any social protection institution.
In addition to unemployment and impediments, another reminder of work in the state is represented by low wages; in the state has the lowest salaries in the country, according to the records of the Mexican Social Welfare and the Ministry of Labor and Social Welfare.
The average monthly income of Sinaloa's workers was 5,352 pesos, much lower than the national average, which is 7,375 pesos.
Abbreviation of FDI
In addition to the decline in economic activivity and a high share of Unemployment in 2011 was the largest outflow of foreign direct investment in the last 10 years, according to the Ministry of the Ministry of Finance, from January to the junior FDI of the state numbered $ 630.697, which shows a fall of 87.74 percent compared to 5,143.12 dollars during the same period in the same period last year.
For its part, the Ministry of economic development of the government of the state recorded only 17.280.000 pesos of investments during the year, which mainly fell on companies from the service sector.
Debt
Amid an economic downscope, the state government was asked to approve a 3-billion pesoc reliance at the state’s Congress, which would be used to invest in infrastructure projects in 18 municipalities to revitalize the economy at the local level, just 2,600 million pesos was approved, and until November there would be no debt benefits in any sector of the state’s economy.
Reaction
For representatives of the business for the first year of the government of Mario López Valdez demonstrates a lack of government capabilities.
Miguel Loredo López, president of the Manufacturing Chamber, said there had been a lack of agreeing the wishes of a variety of sectors during 2011, including the state government itself.
“Efficiuancy in Sinaloa this year was really low, it takes a lot of work to regain positions lost this year, we have to redouble our Effort in 2012, we have not been able to renegotiate the efforts of everyone and, first of all, the good and synchronized work of a new government team, we hope that next year will be much better,” he said.
Squeeze the economy
López Loredo said that events such as early frosts stopped the state's economy this year and its effectiveness and growth were limited.
“Literous volumes of the economy are the result of several events, including frost, one of the main effects was a slow economy, which does not become fast enough to create economic dynamics, which allows you to have an acceptable level of processes, goods and services and, consequently, at the end of the year we close it below our expectations,” he said.
Revision of the Direction
Teodoro Salazar Uriart, president of the Confederation of Employers of the Mexican Republic in Sinaloa, said that the state needs to continue the most dynamic productive activity.
“In general, we have found ourselves working on it, we believe that it is necessary to develop a long-term program that will redefine the direction and economic course of the state, it is necessary to mobilize all the material and human resources that Sinaloa has,” he said.
Upcoming work
President Comparex underscored the importance of denouncing the future direction in order to better guide the government’s efforts.
“On the whole and in general, I would say that the government is trying to be successful in a variety of areas, there is a little progress in terms of economic development, but it is important to develop a long-term program,” he said.
He pointed out that the current administration has demonstrated progress in its own manner of conducting public policies.
“There are facts that allow us to say that the government is starting to get involved in the work and that I can say that some work has been done, and I think the government is aware that there’s a lot more needs to be done,” he said.
