The court will approx the “transition to digital television” in Mexico in 2015
Remoting analog signals with digital signals is one of the most important changes that have overtaken the world in recent years.
The Supreme Judgment has rejected the constitutional claims put forward by the Senate and the House of Representatives against the presidential decree, which transfers the so-called “transition to digital television” from 2021 to 2015, since there have been no eight votes necessary for its annulment.
A minority of the four ministers, including the President of the court, Juan Silva Mesa, considered that President Felipe Calderón had not exceeded his authority by releasing a decree with a detailed description of the actions for the transition to digital terrestrial television.
As a result, the constitutional claims stated by the Senate have been rejected due to the lack of a qualified majority of the eight votes necessary for the court to express the constitutional or unconstitutionality of the decree and remains in force.
The draft statement, prepared by Minister Olga Sánchez Córdero, proposed to declare the decree unconstitutional, because the regulation of the program of radio and vocabulary is the law enshrined in the Federal Telecommunications Commission (Cofetel), in contralumines the principles of hierarchical subordination and legislative reserve.
Only Salvidar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Kordero voted in favor.
According to this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies conducted two constitutional debate to refute the decree and to support the complaints, Minister Sánchez Códero suspended the application of the decree and all the actions referred to it regarding the transition to numerical broadcasting.
At the ministerial meeting, it was also decided to deviate the request of the Chamber of Deputies, without entering into a discussion of the project, also under the responsibility of Minister Sánchez Córdero.
In his speech, the Prime Minister said that he did not agree with the arguments of the project in the sense that the federal executive power could not determine by the general rules of the measure for which the public administration should be prepared.
“I also don’t believe that, if this happened, it would have affected the autonomy of the regulatory body,” and recalled that public policy planning in strategic areas, such as the telecommunications sector, is related to the federal executive, which has a constitutional mandate, the governance of the state.
Replasting analog signals with digital - one of the most important changes that have occurred in recent years has led to a better use of the radio-electric spectrum, better signal quality and more channels.
Laying the transition, he said, would mean postponing the benefits of converting into a digitized form, which involves satisfying societal needs, which has not only a technical dimension, "as it covers economic, social, political, and national security issues."
These questions, he said, “require the work of the whole system, formed on the principle of state economic management, includes a group of authorities, which the Constitution unites into state executive power.”
He reminded that a large number of countries around the world have already completed the transition to digital broadcasting, and this has done this with the help of the executive, despite the presence of highly specialized technical institutions.
From this it cannot be concluded that the government, by adopting the pending decree, which in any case touches the competencies of Congress, ensures in the administrative sphere compliance with the laws adopted by the Legislature, he said.
