The new Italian government will be formed on Wednesday
The appointed prime minister, Mario Monti, will meet with the President of Italy tomorrow to present a new government, which will be faced with a crisis that has put this country on the verge of an economic catastrophe.
Appointed Italian prime minister, Mario Monti, will meet with the President of Italy on Wednesday to present a new government, which will face a crisis that put Italy on the brink of an economic catastrophe and has caused damage to the entire eurozone.
A statement from the presidential palace stated that Monty, appointed on Sunday, will meet with the president, Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday, to confirm that he could form a government.
It is expected that he will present an office compiled mainly from technocrats, although it is not clear when the new governing body will take its positions.
Monty said that he will present to the President the results of his negotiations with political parties in an attempt to form a government.
“I want to correlate my full confidence in the ability of our country to get out of this difficult period,” the former European Commissioner told reporters.
Monty didn’t say bluntly that he was able to form a government, but the tone of his comments showed that the obstacles have been overcome.
“Structure is now well decomposed,” he said.
Monty has completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, which is much less than usual, since Italy has struggled with the political and financial crisis, which brought their debt to a critical level
The administration led by former European Commissioner Monti must approve the austerity package demanded by European leaders to regain confidence in Italy.
At the heart of the pressure on Monty, in order for him to hasten, unstable markets will set income from BTP bonds for 10 years more than 7 per cent, the level at which Greece and Ireland had to be rescued.
Emma Marchegalia, the leader of the Italian Federation of Employers, told reporters after meeting with Monti: “We said that we will actively support this government.”
We believe that this government is Italy's last chance to get out of this critical jeopardy.
Monty’s chances of success were largely based on the support of LDP party Silvio Berlusconi, who was forced to resign on Saturday because of the crisis.
“We believe that Professor Monti’s efforts should lead to a good outcome,” Angelino Alfano told reporters, a centrist law secretary.
LDP support, Italy's largest party, is important because so far many of its members have opposed the government's technocrats that Monti forms.
Parliamentary support
Monti’s new government must have strong parliamentary support to carry out reforms, which are probably unpopulous austerity reforms.
Any blocking or escaping of these attempts can lead to a new and destructive attack on financial markets.
The Italian association of foreign banks increased the pressure, arranging that Monti’s failure could be a disaster.
Monty began on Monday his consultations with political parties, alliances and business groups, as well as with youth and women's organizations.
He will end a series of meetings on Tuesday evening.
Monty was assigned on Sunday Napolitano who allowed an extremely rapid transition in response to the crisis.
After a short remnatch late last week, when it became clear that Berlusconi would retire, Italy’s debiture has now reached a critical level amid uncertainty about whether the new prime minister would be a success.
