On November 4 criminologist of CTI Cali went down in history as a person who confirmed the identity of FARC CEO Alfonso Kano by comparing his fingerprints.
Of the seven criminologists working at the CTI, this week it was the guild to “dress” Elizar Gonzalez, a man from Puerto Tejada, who spent 21-Year-consecret.
At the beginning of his career, Gonsales worked in the cities of Choco, where he often had to sneak through the jungle to reach a place where there were bodies that had to be identified.
In 2000, he moved to Vallia and after graduating from relevant courses he became a specialist in the field of forensics, where he studied friction of the skin, i.e. palms and walls.
In an Elysard operation week to identify a person known as Alfonso Cano, he was told to take his clothes for no more than three days.
Something great was approaching.
On Friday at night he arrived by helicopter from Kauk to Palmyra.
He knew that he would need to identify two corpses, and one was supposed to belonged to a man who led the world's oldest guerrilla war, so that they asked him to take ten photographs of Guillermo Leon Sayens (his real name).
However, when he arrived with a research team at the place where the body was located, he did not identify it by the appearance; he was very much different from that shown in the mass media.
He was without beard, dressed as a local farmer, without glasses and much thinner.
Fingerprint doesn't lie.
Using a lucrative deconvene, ink, a shoulder blade and lanthank, Elizar matched the fingertips of the corpse with those that were in the pictures.
His eyes didn't fail: it was Alfonso Kano.
On the same night, the representative of Puerto Tejad was asked to confirm the defense Minister Juan Carlos Pinson that it was the head of the Federal Customs Union
Fingerfinger Fingerprint Search
Recognition from Kali’s Sijin, President Juan Manuel Santos Elishar Gonzales at a press conference on Operation Kano, is especially significant.
Many people don't know our work.
Danexposcopy is a tantal state, without blaws, it can play a key role in solving crimes.
In addition to the identification of the bodies, fingerprint specialists take part in the investigation of crimes, such as the theft and murder, and establish the identity of the detainees.
When detained, many criminals have fake certificates or do not have documents.
We take their imprints and look for them in the database.
The researcher said that in the case of the the theft, he had to carry a piece of the wall of the house, because in this place the thief relied with his hands to seize a safe full of jewelry and money.
It’s difficult to work with the walls, because they are not comfortable surfaces for removing fingerprints, but in this case we can take a picture of the whole palm of your palm and establish the identity of a criminal who took part in several thefts,” he says.
Fingerprints also help to excluding or lodging suspects in homicide-affiliated cases.
Recently was found a dead man with bottles of liquor around him.
During the investigation, it was established that a relative could be suspected of committing a crime and, in fact, the fingerprints on the bottle coincided.
While this doesn't guarantee that he was a killer, it proves that he was at the scene of the murder.
A specialist in datyloscopy from CTI explained that a chemical or physical reagent is applied to places where there are imprints.
When they appear, the shoulder bulb or tambrite is used to remove them.
After that, fingerprint pictures are sent to Asif, which is a database of criminals, or entered in the register.
“It’s a problem that we still have a lot of undocumented Columbians,” a CTI expert said.
He explained that not everything is as shown in the CSI program.
Just like not all the gold shining, not everything that you touch leaves imprints.
However, if this is possible, then the comparison is performed.
