Japan, revived after an Earthquoia
GDP grew 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption and put an end to the turn of the fall, which began in March
Since March last year the Japanese economy has been paralyzed and is still recovering from a devastating earthquake that caused a nuclear crisis, the most terrible catastrophe suffered by the Land of the Rising Sang since the Second World War
According to the first estimates of the Tokyo government, material losses accounted for $ 300,000 million - the blow, which, despite the difficult world situation, it seems to be passing.
After three unscorched quarters in a row, data on the gross domestic product from July to September definitely helped to float the page: GDP grew by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and 6% year-large.
Registered in the second Quarter - for the Japanese financial year begins in March 2012 - also embodies the fastest growth rates from January to March 2010.
The renouncement can be partly explained by the resurgence of the purchase of machine equipment to restore areas devastated by the earthquake and subsequent tsunami.
In fact, the companies increased their investments by 1.1% against a 0.9% reduction in capital costs, which was observed from January to March, the first decrease after six months of uninterrupted growth.
However, a real turning point in the Japanese economy is not based solely on the Keynesian impulse, which calls to take advantage of the crisis to strengthen - or reorganize the infrastructure, using flaws to grow from the ground.
Japan has wretched, mainly due to the development of household consumption, which now accounts for 60% of Japanese GDP.
This indicator increased by 1% under the influence of an increase in the cost of cars, travel and recreation.
Stigma of the yen
Tokyo provided the internal market, when the yen - the Japanese currency - has become one of the harbouring currencies, chosen by investors who want to get rid of the debt crisis in the euro zone and negative forecasts regarding the United States, a privilege that puts, like a stone, on the accounts of exporting companies.
With a stronger currency, the products are expensive abroad, which is a serious blow to the country whose economic course is determined by the health of large technology companies.
Tuyota or Sonny, for example, blame the yen for their lowest levels from July to September.
While the markets continue the siege of peripheral EU countries, Yoshihiko Noda government does not reduce vigilance.
We should be attentive to persistent risks, such as the deterioration of certain foreign economies, the impact of the floods in Thailand and a sharp increase in yen's value, Motohisa Furuqawa said yesterday.
In fact, the prospects are not inspiring.
The Organization for Economic Co-Columbarity and Development (OECD) reported that the majority of the participating countries have shown clear signs of a decline in economic inactivity in September.
An indicuator, which predicts fractures in the global economy, showed that the main forces are slowing.
Then it fell on four tenths to 100 .4 points, compared with 100.8 in August.
The eurozone has even fallen below an average, which is at the level of 100 points, after it collapsed on eight tenths to 99.13 points.
