Lady Sophia: "These are instruments of progress and hope"
The queen stressed the “basic” place of Spain in relation to this type of finance
“Spain took a prominent place in the world in microfinancing.”
Korin Sophia recalled yesterday that in the last two decades the country became the second-largest donation with regard to the instruments of collaborator whose “essentiality”, she said, this is “an instrument of social progress, justice and hopes for a better future of all of humanity, based on faith in people”.
The Queen opened yesterday the Fifth World Summit on Microdiluence in Valladolid with the belief that “There is no doubt”, that such actions “absolutely necessary”, to achieve the millennium goals set by the United Nations by 2015, and “primarily by halving the halving the number of people living less than one dollar a day – the level at which the line of poverty is recorded.
Spain “still is still working”, to expand the network of beneficiaries around the world and undertakes this task with all “responsiveness”, says Doña Sophia and hopes that the efforts and achievements that have arisen during the summit, which will continue until Thursday, will “provide the creation of new abilities and best opportunities for the poorest people, and especially for women, “the basic recipients of these loans”.
There are "many achievements" and "many difficulties we face."
The micro-looccupimentation summit becomes not only a platform for disputes, but also a platform that promotes “two main goals”.
In this way, Her Majesty explained that she hopes that by the end of 2015, 175 million poor families will take advantage of these financial services and a hundred millions of these households will be taken out of the poverty in which they are immersed.
Impetus to the creation of a microloaning system in Spain and around the rest of the world "was on the way begun years ago" by Muhammad Yunus, Ms. Sophia emphasized as "an act of justice and recognition".
And she described the father of this instrument as an “amazing meladimist in the universal cause of fighting poverty”, whose work is known for 15 years since the time the queen went to Bangladesh to study his lending policy.
A year a year later, the first World’s World Summit in D.Does and other meetings, such as those held in Côte d’Ivoire in 1999, Canada in 2006, and “everywhere I have seen continuous development”, said Her Majesty, whose connection with this project brought her yesterday the title of “challed head of the family”, collected in Valladolid “banker of the Poor”.
At the outgo, Queen Sophia culminated, urging those present to participate in the discussion and find solutions to move forward in the fight against poverty.
"Ethical Imperative"
Foreign Minister Trinidad Jiménez said that solidarity is an “ethical imper imperative” for every democratic state.
In this connection, she said that it would be a mistake to associate cooperation with developing countries at times of the economic boom, since this is a “constitutional commitment”.
Jiménez concluded that microloitation implies a “serious and firm course aimed at eradicating poverty”, and now “largest than at any time” the governments of the developed countries should work according to the criteria of solidarity and prevent it from becoming a victim of spending cuts.
For Spain, micro-looccupimentation is "one of the main areas of cooperation for the sake of development."
The Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodríguez, praised the “success” in giving “confidence” to people “deprived, before whom all the doors are closed”, and the mayor of Valladolid Francisco Javier León de la Riva greeted everyone present in the city, which “opens its doors covered by the spirit of solidarity”.
The CDU proposes to elect the President of the European Commission with a universal vote
The first day of the Christian-Democranic Union (CDU) Congress on Monday, November 14 in Leipzig, passed without significant problems for Chancellor Angela Merkel, who is also the chairman of the Christian democratic party.
The future of Europe was not originally planned in the program, but it arose itself in the autumn, in particular, to satisfy activists who believe that the government's policy is not as strict as it should, today in relation to Greece and, perhaps, tomorrow in relation to Italy.
The draft resolution on the Presidency of the European Commission, submitted for discussion by the leadership, was adopted almost unanimously.
Of the 1001 delegates, only nine voted against and ten abstained.
The vouching, which lasted a few minutes, which deserved their speeches of Angela Merkel and a few hours later Wolfgang Scheibla, the Europhilet and the Minister of the Treasury, left no room for doubt.
The main idea of the chancellor and the draft of the draft resolution is expressed in the slogan: the correct answer to the crisis - "no less than Europe, but more than Europe".
Not only because Europe means the world but because “nine-million-million jobs depend directly on the euro.”
For the chancellor "the time has come for a breakthrough in Europe."
This may mean changes in the Lisbon Treaty, which make it possible to strengthen the control of the European Union over the financial policies of the eurozone countries, but it may also have an impact on the election of the President of the European Commission with a universal suffrage “to give Europe a person”.
In any case, this is one of the proposals of the draft resolution adopted by the Congress, the idea of which obviously belongs to Wolfgang Schaibla.
Of course, it is unlikely that twenty-seven countries will quickly agree with this decision; the UK is opposed to this, and CDU leaders believe that Nikolay Sarkozy is also against it.
But the soil is prepared.
In the same same way, the delegates overwhelmingly rejected the proposal to weigh the vote of each EU member state on the European Central Bank, in accordance with the size of the economy of each of them, which would noticeably increase the weight of the German economy.
Desiring to reassure the situation in his party, the chancellor increased the number of links to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl, but she did not seek to influence Congress at the expense of “small” European countries or countries in a pampered situation who like to criticize “arrogant” Germany.
Although the draft resolution on Europe devotes a whole paragraph to the importance of "German-French friendship", Angela Merkel did not mention France.
Only in passing.
Remembrance that two centuries ago Leipzig became the stage of a bloody battle.
In fact, this was one of the most humiliating defeats of the Napoleonic army.
What Is Hospital Sheet Fraud?
Following monetary allowances, UMP and the government began an attack on hospitals.
Nikolay Sarkozy arrives in Bordeaux on November 15 to visit the Family Benefiries Foundation and deliver a speech on this issue, which has been periodically raised since 2007, about the fight against fraud in the field of social benefits.
And to help this offset, which arose out of a desire to save in a difficult situation, but also a part of the right-to-re-e-election campaign, the majority wants to continuo on sick leave.
First of all, bearing in view of budget savings, the government intends to impose on employees of private-traffic companies for the fourth day of unpaid leave before the payment of benefits.
Obviously, without the special consent of the Employer, the affected worker will lose wages for four days, equal to at least 15% of his/her monthly income.
Measure, which jeopardizes a slumber of rage, but which, according to Les Echos, will lead to savings of 280 million euros.
And which will be accompanied by other measures with a more symbolic effect, namely to impose on civil servants one day without the payment of sickness benefits (public employees still did not have to wait for the payment of benefits, as opposed to a three-day waiting for private-sector workers).
At a time when these issues are being debated, the majority also re-raise the topic of fraud, bearing in view of thebusing of hospital sheets.
“Control will become more stringent in addition, if you are caught, you will have to repay all the payments you illegally received,” Bertran warned Kssavi Bertran on the RTL on Saturday, November 13.
As in other cases, the effect of the application was somewhat disproportionate to the real abuse of sick leave.
They have indeed been undergoing constant scrutiny and political attacks that bear fruit since 2002.
People in France don’t get sick more than anywhere else in Europe.
Let's first look at the numbers in the country.
According to the National Health Insurviorance Fund (CNAM), more than €237 million for daily compensation was paid in 2006.
According to the Institute for Research and Dividing of the Health Care Economics, the daily compensatory expenses in 2008, amounting to 11.3 thousand million euros accounted for 5% of the total spending on health.
Of this amount, 46% belonged to maternity leave and industrial accidents and 54% for sickness benefits, which amounted to 6.2 thousand million euros.
And, therefore, amounted to 2.5% of the costs associated with health.
The French spent an averaging 14.5 days on sick leave in 2010, compared with 17.8 days in 2009, according to a study by the Alma Consulting Group.
An arithmetic average that hides some deficiencies; another study, published in 2007 by Monster.com's career management site, found that 75% of the 40 000 French workers surveyed said they didn't take a single hospital day.
Another study, this time related to health insurance, claims that in 2010, 37% of hospitals lasted less than 8 days, 15% from 15 days to one month, 15% from one to three months and 11% lasted longer.
If you compare these figures with the numbers of European neighbors, it is clear that French workers are no more inclined to take a hospital than employees of any other country.
A long-term study conducted in 2010 by CNRS researchers showed that between 1994 and 2001 the total lack of work in France (for health reasons) ranged between 10% and 11 per cent, compared to 20-28 per cent in Denmark, 15% in the United Kingdom and 16-18% in the Netherlands.
Fraud associated with hospital sheets is very low compared to “gray” employment.
As for the level of scam, it is very low.
Tougher legislation after 2002 led to a systemic examination of sick leave for more than 45 days.
In 2008, CNAM found that 13% of the 2,85 000 short-term illness conclusions were “unjustified” or too long, accounting for 37,050.
In the case of systematically verifiably inspected sick leave for a period of more than 45 days, 11 per cent cases out of 1.2 million were “inappropriate or unreasonable”, which is equal to 132 000.
In general, it turns out that 169 000 cases were “unreasonable” for several million employees who received sick leave.
Difficulties, which show very a few cases compared, for example, to the figures of “gray” employment.
According to UMP Deputy Dominic Tiana, the author of the report on this issue in June, they amount from 9 to 15 million euros of lost budget revenues per year, which exceeds the total expenses for daily compensation, and, consequently, infinitely higher than the loss from fraud.
Papadimos: Greece should follow a new settlement plan
Greece will need a new settlement program to save its economy.
Prime Minister Lukas Papadimos stated this on November 14 in the parliament, adds, adding that the 100 days granted to his coalition government were not enough to carry out this task.
“In order to achieve the goal of recapturing the economy, we need the support of our European partners and a new program of budget adjustments,” the head of the government said, who made his first public statement, unveiling a discussion on a vote of a vote of confidence in Parliament on Wednesday.
Implement the decisions made at the Eurozone summit on October 27 should be the “basic task” of the new government, since the country’s presence in the Eurozone is “attached”.
Respectively, Papadimos predicts a decrease in the country's budget deficit "approximately 9%" of GDP by the end of the year, at 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
The right rejected the measures of the new government
Antonis Samaras, the right-wing leader on Monday, expressed his support for the measures already adopted to try to get out of the debt crisis, but warned that his party did not support additional austerity measures.
“We won’t vote for the new measures,” he said at a meeting with MPs from his party New Democrat.
He expressed his agreement with the tasks of reducing the deficit and the debt and the fight against embezzlement, as well as his rejection of any course that prevents the recovery of the economy.
Olli Rehn, the European commissioner responsible for the economy and finance, who has warned that the IMF and the EU will not unblock a necessary loan of 8 thousand million euros without written guarantees from all the parties that they will support the planned measures, Antonio Samaras replied that his words were enough and that he would not sign anything under external pressure.
Euthanasia and self-help must be legalized, the expert committee says
Canads Refuse to Detention According to the Report of the Royal Society of Canada
As for palliative medicine, the committee argues that governments, health institutes and doctors are working together to provide best palliative care, not counting cases of cancer.
Canadians refuse to die.
Only thircle th of Canadians are willing to tell their doctor when they want to die and take steps in this direction.
This is one of the inferiences of an impressive report of the Royal Society of Canada, which was attended by six experts from different fields, including ethic, law and medicine.
The Committee invites the Government to change the penal code so as to authorize suicide with outside help and euthanalysis, when this choice is imposed by circumstance, and the patient can be considered competent for such a decision.
To begin with, experts cite a recent study of the Economic Expert Bureau, which compared the quality of death in 40 countries around the world.
Although Canada ranks 10, the study notes that “the mechanization of death in Canada has created a culture in which people are afraid to arguing about death.”
Due to the medical situation or not, but 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, according to experts appointed by the Royal Society of Canada.
And 68.6% of them ends with death in the hospital.
The country's highest figure in Quebec, where 86% of deaths occur in the hospital.
On the delicate issue of euthanasia and suicide with external assistance, the authors of the report come to a conclusion, which contravened the position of the federal government, which has already stated that it does not want to change anything in the criminal code against euthanasia and suicide with outside help.
Now, according to experts who have also studied the position of all countries where such actions have been legalized or decriminalized, there is no evidence that this decriminalization entails abuse of use, that is, euthanasia or suicide with outside help against people who are unable or unwilling to take such a decision.
On the contrary, Jocelyn Doughni confirmed during a teleconference, who presented the report, the majority of such cases were in countries where euthanasia and self-accepted suicide have not been legalized.
Euthanasia is practised in Canada, where it is obviously illegal.
Suicidia with outside use is practiced in Canada, where it is obviously illegal, explains Joseline Douney.
The Canadian for Euthanasia
It should be remembered that 85% of Canadians support euthanasia, because, according to a survey by Angus Reid in 2010, they believe that this will allow people at the ends of their lives to alleviate their suffering.
And 66% believe that legalizing euthanasia won’t be a signal that the lives of older people or persons with disabilities are less valuable.
As for people who assisted in suicidal, 41% of the respondents believe that they should not be prosecuted.
As for the extension or termination of medical care for the patient, the situation is less unequivourable, the authors of the report say.
They also suggested, in addition to other recommendations, that the penal code must stipulate that a legally verifiable cessation of medical care is not a crime.
The committee of experts also demands that health professionals be trained, in a spirit of respect for the abandonment of medical care at the end of their lives without fear of criminal prosecution.
With regard to palliative care, the committee calls on the government, health-care institutions and doctors to work together to develop the best possible palliative care and not only in cases of cancer.
Finally, with respect to palliative or terminal sedation already widely used in Canadian health institutions, the committee requires it to be used when it does not serve to blunt physical pain as euthanasia and are subjected to the same restrictions.
Observations of other experts
Some health-related legal experts have already commented on the conclusion of a report by the Royal Society of Canada.
meter Pierre DeShan, an expert on legislation in the field of health, described the “extremegitude” position of the experts who signed the report, because, among other things, it does not limit euthanasia the use of euthanasia with fatalities.
This opens the door for a situation where anyone who is tired of life and who is more than 18 years old can ask for help in committing a suicidal, says Deschán meter, who discussed the summaries of the report yesterday.
Deschamps also stressed that the authors of the report, it seems, put the independence of the individual above all the other values that make up the social fabric of the social fabric of society.
He said that "there are warning signs and prohibitions in society."
Margaret Somerville, an expert on health laws, also expresses “categorical disagreement with the conclusions of the report, which she described as a “manifesto for euthanasia”.
Margaret Somerville, who herself is a member of the Royal Society of Canada, disputes, in particular, these reports regarding abuse in certain countries where euthanasia and suicide are legalized, as in the Netherlands and Oregon.
