The Russian Scientist helped the Iranian nuclear program
Russian scientist Vyacheslav Danilenko helped Iran to develop a detonator, suitable for use in nuclear weapons, the U.S. agency specializing in nuclear hazard reported on Monday.
The Institute of Science and International Security (ISIS) draws on a recent report of the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) and other documents of the United Nations agency in order to identify this scientist.
Last week, the IAEA expressed concern about “possible military aspect” of the Iranian nuclear program
According to ISIS, relying largely on IAEA documents, Mr. Danilenko was born in 1934, worked for 30 years, “since since the sixties, at the Soviet military nuclear center in Chelyabinsk (Urals) and was involved in the production of synthestic diamond methods of explosion.”
In 1989 or 1991, he resigned from the center to set up a company that produced “Nanodiamonds” in Kiev.
The financial difficulties of his company led him into contact with the Iranian embassy in Ukraine in 1995.
He then cooperated with the Iranian program from 1996 to 2002, until he returned to Russia
In its IAEA’s report mentions “clear indications that the development of Iran’s nuclear detonator system “contributed to the work of a foreign expert, who not only knew this technique well, but who also informed the agency that it worked for most of his career on this technology as part of his home country’s military nuclear program.”
Anti-Muslim Offensive Offensive Includes Increased by 50%
According to statistics published on Monday by the FBI between 2009 and 2010, anti-Muslim crimes increased by almost 50%, while during the same period other forms of violence on racial and religious grounds decreased slightly or did not change.
According to these figures, the total number of cases of violence against Muslims has risen from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, an increase of 49%, compared with an increase of 1,3 per cent violence against Catholics, a reduction of 4% attacks on Jews and a total increase of 14 per cent in religious crimes.
The total number of crimes due to hid, according to the FBI, increased very slightly to 6,628 cases, of which 47.3% were motivated by racial disparities and 20 per cent religious disparities.
After the decline in 2009, it raises a concern that the number of these crimes is increasing, emphasizes the human human rights organization Human Rights First “increased cases of violence against Muslims is especially significant”, the organization adds in an official statement.
"Human Rights First has long stressed that acts of violence against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crimes, should be seen as serious violations of human human rights," added one of the organization's leaders, Paul Lejandron.
“The U.S. government can and should do more to cope with these crimes, he adds, believing it can be done by improving police reports on hate crimes.”
“Chatage crimes” in the federal law are the legacy of the period of the struggle for civil rights.
This law was passed after the death of Martin Luther King to penalize acts of violence associated with race, skin color, religion, nationality, and today's sexual orientation.
