Austria
Introducing the “great-great-great-great-trip” of the
The leadership of the large coalition of Social Democrats and Christian Democrats, who is in power in Austria, decided on Tuesday to register a budget “golden rule” in the constitution in order to reduce the budget deficit and thus to avoid a possible reduction by rating agencies of the state credit rating of Austria (threutennee).
Social Democrat Chancellor Werner Feimann personally declared this decision at the end of the meeting of the Council of Ministers, despite the fact that the Austrian public debt is 74.6%, which is much higher than the upper norm threshold of 60% established by the Maastricht Treaty but is lower than the public debt of such countries as Italy, Greece or Spain.
If Austria’s solvencies fell by only one point, with A.A. before AA+, we would have to pay interest three thousand thousand million euros more every year, the vice-Chancellor and foreign minister from Christian Democrat Michael Spindelegher gave an argument.
Thus, Austria complied with the demand of the summit of the member countries of the Euro zone on October 26 in Brussels; the heads of state and governments demanded a reduction in budget deficits by the end of 2012 and the return to balanced public accounts through a constitution or engniered law.
The decree, adopted by the Austrian government, reminiscent of the “German model”, since Germany was the first European country to accept this “rule”.
Until 2017, the structural deficit of the state state’s state-dollar budget should be reduced every year by 0.75% of the gross domestic product (GDP), and from 2017 the structural deficit of the state budget of a federated state in any year should not exceed 0.35% of GDP, and land, like communes, should represent balanced budgets.
The goal is to force public adequacy to fall below the mark of 60% enshrined in the Maastricht Treaty by 2020/2021.
Esikhiko Noda
Threatening New Interference in the Foreign Exchange
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that the Japanese authorities will carry out new interventions in the foreign exchange market, if the yen's value continues to rise.
We will intercede, as we have done this last time, if we decend the excessive volatility of the yen's exchange rate, explained Mr. Noda to the Senate.
The yen rose almost to a historical mark that prompted the Japanese authorities to intervene on October 31.
On this day, Tokyo smuggled the yen for dollars on a large scale in order to reduce the value of the Japanese currency, which has set a new record of strength since 1945.
But since then the yen has grown all the time-constituted, pursued by the purchases of investors, who perceive it as an “sanctuary” in these vague times of the fall of the economy around the world and the debate crisis in Europe.
The Dollar fell below 77 yen on Tuesday, and the euro fell below the symbolic bar of 105 yen, close to the level that triggered the latest intervention.
On the eve eve of the financial crisis of 2008-2009, the dollar valued about 120 yen, and the euro more than 160 yen.
The growth of the Japanese currency is detrimental of Japanese exports, as it makes Japanese products more expensive and reduces the income of Japanese firms from abroad by converting to the national currency.
“Jeropia is Fighting to Rebuild” after the March 11 tsunami that devastated the Tohoku (north-in-octrusion), said Mr. Noda, who fears that yena’s growth would kill the fragile germ of recovery.
The world's third-largest economic power resumed growth in the third Quarter -1.5% compared to the previous quarter - for the first time in a year, thanks to the increase in exports and the consumption of households affected by the disaster
But the Prime Minister feels that the high yen price does not reflect the "fundamental economic indicators of Japan".
Finance Minister Jün Azummi called on the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which opened a two-to-day meeting of its monetary policy council on Tuesday, to take all necessary steps towards easing the yen.
“Hirohida Yamaguchi’s deputy agency has warned that the Bank of Japan has already taken “relevant steps”, making it clear that no additional easing on Wednesday should not be expected.
At the last meeting at the end of October, the emplating organization increased the purchase of treasury bonds by 5.000 million yen to 5.000 million yen (519 thousand million euros), the ceiling of the amount that it allocates for the purchase of various financial instruments to saturate the securities market.
