Energy Politics:
Evidence Must Preclude Dominated by Dogmas
If there is a topic that can not be decided after a brief discussion or under the influence of emotions, it is an energy policy.
Availability and the cost of electricity directly affects the purchasing power and competitiveness of companies.
This is a healthy tendency that electoral meetings become a reason to reflect on the share and place occupied by nuclear energy.
Controversy does not bear fruit until all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options are studied.
France should build its opinion on objective facts and on rational data.
Now we are 7 thousand million people on the planet, the demographic development will entail a doubling of the need for electricity by 2050, unless a huge part of the world population is devoid of vital elements.
During the same period, it will become necessary to fill the increasing shortage of fossil resources in order to continue the production of electricity constantly, without forgetting the crucial need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions in order to combat the climate imbalance - a theme that has vanished from an environmental debate.
Accident in Fukushima doesn't change any of these.
That’s why Germany is left alone in its decision to abandon nuclear energy.
This is not the case of Belgium, which puts its solution in dependence on the need to find a source able to replace nuclear energy, or Switzerland, which intends to build only the most state-of-of-the-art reactors.
On the other hand, Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, China, India, Brazia are only a few of those who resolutely continue their projects.
The United United States decided after Fukushima to add one installation to its kit, resuming the construction of the station, which was interrupted after an accident on the Tri-Mile Island.
“Although a German-based model based on renewable energy is sometimes cited as an example, the reality is contrary to this argument.
The choice of Berlin will result from an increase in the cost of electricity, an increased energy dependency exacerbated by an increased need for imported gas, in particular from Russia, a surge in carbon dioxide emissions due to the construction of gas and coal-mounted stations.
In a few few months, all these unpleasant effects will already be noticeable.
The example of Denmark should look forward.
The European Turbine-Consider-Building Champion, from which it receives 30% of its electricity, is also the largest consumer of coal and gas due to intermittent-guided-guided-winning winds; resulting in carbon dioxide emissions and electricity prices are 65% and 50% higher, respectively.
On the other hand, thanks to the constancy of our energy policy, our neighbors pay 40% more for electricity than French households.
Since in a galbalized economy, the price of energy is a key factor of competitiveness and collateral for the maintenance of industrial equipment in the national territory, French companies are benefiting from the cheapest electricity in Europe.
As a result, any significant decrease in nuclear energy will lead to a large increase in the cost of electricity, which will illusory the beautiful statements about the new industrialization of our country and the protection of purchasing power.
The price that the majority of the French refuse to pay, especially because they don’t object to this type of energy in principle.
It should be remembered that the energy policy of the country made it possible to create an industrial industry in 125 000 direct workplaces and 410 000 mediated.
Strongly export-focus, it attracts a whole galaxy of small and medium-sized enterprises specialized in international commerce and creates many jobs that cannot be relocated to a different industry.
If France decided to abandon nuclear energy, it would have to sabotage a large number of these enterprises and lost 6 thousand million euros a year from the French export of nuclear equipment and services.
Which electrical engineer to order an EPR, calculated to work for sixty years, if the cliff of its manufacturer is planned?
It must be recognized that there are no divided or diabolical sources of energy.
The development by Areva of solutions with low emissions of carbon dioxide, nuclear and renewable, indicates the complementarity of energy sources.
For the nuclear energy to maintain the highest level of security is undoubtedly a vital issue
In France, we have a security body that exercises uncompromising and transparent control of existing energy centers such as Flamanwille.
Our teams, whose professionalism is universally recognized, are filled with the same obstetric security.
Within 50 years the nuclear industry experienced three serious incidents.
Only one, Tri-Mile-Eyland, occurred under normal conditions of operation, but without any consequences for a person or the environment
In Chernobyl, the device of the reactor and an unforgivable human error led to a tragedy.
In Fukushima, some people seem to forget that the accident occurred as a result of two natural disasters of unparalleled forces, which entailed the deaths of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear industry will draw lessons from this accident, as it extracted from the previous two.
The fruit of this step and co-operation between the security authorities, operators and the French and German constructors, EPR, is a project to withstand such phenomena.
Since it is regarded by all experts in the field of energy wishing to build a reactor, stopping its construction in Flamanville will be a great gift for competitors Areva and EDF, calculivating the path to gaining leadership in the inevitable development of nuclear energy.
It is also necessary to legally look for the best combination of energy for our country, but it would be irresponsible to allow emotions, dogmas and preconceivable manoeuvres to prevail in a dispute, which is so important for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Immigrania - a burden or good for the economy?
Nikolay Sarkozy’s immigratory policy has been criticised from a variety of parties – the overloading of police, judicial and administrative services subordinate to the policy and the interoperability of this policy with the self-proceded status of France as “human rights states”.
Then there was an administrative harassation of foreign students, which was included in the news, when the Minister of internal Affairs (“Liebersion” on May 23) stated that France “does not need foreign talents, masons and waiters”.
But it is very seldom analyzed from an economic point of view from an economic point of view.
Left-and-female agrees a lot on this issue.
On the part of the right-wing tone, Jacques Schirak, who in 1976 stated that “900 000 unemployed will not be a problem in the country with 2 million foreign workers”, but on the left – Michel Rocard, who explained in 1990 that France “can not accept the poor from all over the world”.
Difficulty in generosity
In the year of 2005, the fear of invadation to the territory of the country hortiday porcelievers felt both the Left and on the right.
Both sides see immigration a burden for the French economy and for French society.
Difficulty only in the level of generosity that they will persecute for immigrants.
François Oll'lland limited himself to cornices, saying that “joint work together will solve our problem of immigration.”
It’s an idea of immigranania as a burden and a problem can provide an electoral advantage, but it’s economically very expensive.
As economists Izan Goldin and Jeffrey Cameron reminiscent to us in a recent book summarizing the latest data on immigrant (Special people: How migration has shaped our world and will determine our future, May 2011, Princeton University Publishing, 352 p.), there is a broad consensus among professionals about the positive impact of migranium flows on economic growth, wages and employment in countries that accept immigrants.
Restriction of immigration leads to a weakening of growth and reduces employment.
Fears before the influencies of immigrant are based on the notion that they are able to replace local workers, especially low-skilled, provoking a decrease in wages.
But the experience shows that in reality, immigrants are much more complemented than replacing local labor, performing works that are lacking local labor.
Unqualified immigrants are working in sectors that don’t attract local workforce, but the most skilled in dynamic sectors where learning is not related to job offers.
Possible Influence on Salary
Just as it would be difficult for the surgeon to work in a country where anesthesiologists is a shortage of anesthesiologists, the complement that exists between local and immigrant causes the arrival of immigrants to positively affect the salaries of local workers.
Giovanni Perry estimated that an increase of 1% of the migratory flow leads to an increase of 0.6-0.9% in real long-term salaries.
And this does not take into account the fact that immigrant-contained diversity contributes to the creation of new ideas and economic growth (among American Nobel laureates there is a large share of naturalized immigrants, and Google, Intel, Paypal and Yahoo were all founded by immigrants).
Migrants also bring a net profit in social security, giving an average of 1% of the total budgets of European countries.
According to the World Labour Organization, for example, in Germany an immigrant, having arrived at the age of 30 at the age of 30, during their lifetime will bring a state budget a net profit (revenue minus expenses), an average of 150,000 euros.
Nicolas Sarkozy at the beginning of his mandate was going to clumble into the missing points of growth.
Mentioned teeth, which were used to scare away immigrants, gave exactly the exact opposite effect.
“Euro Instituted Democracy, Refuses to Comprehend European Democrat
Democracie is a valuable acquisition; but at the same time fragile.
The European Continent should remember that, having invented it in Athens more than two thousand years ago, in the Twentieth Centuries it experienced the tragedies of World War I, totalitarian regimes, the Holocaust, the Goulaga, as well as Franko, Salazar in Portugal and colonists in Greece.
Some believed that in order for democracie to finally triumph over its enemies, it is enough to arrange a vote and make it as direct as possible.
Since the time of Napoleon, "Small" it is known that this comes from the superficiality of thinking.
Universal voting, although an indispensable condition of democracy, is not a sufficient prematuration and may even become an “instrument of suppression”, as the Republican Philosophy Étienn Vashro once said.
No, democracy also necessitates much more - a public space, a public spirit, values, an organization, the separation and control of the authorities, education, the Enlightenment, economic and social sorul, justice.
The European crisis that we are going through is an economic crisis, but it is primarily a political crisis and a crisis of democracy.
In this regard, the palinology of the truth-false-false Greek referendum will have a double reveling effect, demonstrating both the need to seriously change policy and an unpleasant sense of its impossibility and inconsistencies.
This ambivalence needs to comprehend in order to overcome.
The point is that the front line of the struggle for democracy has now shifted and, according to many, republican soldiers are aiming now in the wrong direction.
We won't win anything by demanding a series of national referenda convened as needed and chaotically.
"Maint-blanch" for an absolutely European monarch or a leap with ties to the abyss of nationalism - what is the choice for a citizen!
No, we don’t dream of guillotine blasphemy, we are dreaming of an guillotine blasphemy, collectively and consolidated – choices and democracy, this time at a pan-Turkey level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal, and it will not matter how many of them you will have to enumerate, because there is only one crisis - a crisis of Europe.
The whole of Europe complies entirely with the dictatorship of the markets and rating agencies, suffers from its weakness and from a lack of solidarity.
The whole of Europe humiliated is humiliated on an international stage.
Many are worried about the transfer of sovereignty to Europe, but the whole of Europe gradually loses its own sovereignty, and each of its nations.
It is necessary to urgently restore the management of the European project under popular sovereignty.
Because it’s only joint work that Europeans will be able to reduce everyone’s debate, get rid of the markets and prepare for the future, investing in it.
The way out of the crisis will require solutions at the pan-European level, and they can not be taken without complete democratic legitimacy, without leading us to a catastrophe.
After a lengthy discussion of this proposal of the socialists and Jacques GODora, everyone now demands a European economic government.
Let's do this.
But imagining it without a stronger more economic, political and democratic integration, which has come to life, is another insanguinity and a new stalemate.
We need to start a new stage of European construction.
To establish the republic in France, it was necessary to “create” the republicans.
A reversal of interests would not be enough.
This lesson remains in force.
We need to build a Europe that is united at the economic level.
This can be done only by creating a more democratic Europe at a political level, a real popular European sovereignty.
In order to create a Europe, let's create Europeans.
Contactless chip cards are distributed more and more broadly.
The world exhibition of chip-cards opens on Tuesday in Wilpent near Paris in an atmosphere of optimism.
In fact, 2011 will culminate in 11% growth of world sales of microprocessors (bank cards, phones with SIM-cards), exceeding, according to Eurosmart, 6 thousand million units.
The most important sector of non-contact technologies, which allows you to pay for public transport or purchases by simply passing a card over a reader.
460 million non-contact-free chip cards will be traded in the world this year.
Their share will increase in 2012 (580 million units awaited) and will account for almost 10% of the global market of chip cards (6.925 million cards).
Europe, lagging behind the Asia-Actober region for a long time, is now moving forward with face-to-contact payments.
Approximately 26 million Visa bank cards have already been equipped with this function, and they are accepted by 75 000 terminals.
France will start from a more modest position: 400,000 contact-free Visa cards are in circulation.
But large stores increase the pace.
Karrefour released 2.5 million contactless Pass MasterCard.
In order to switch to a higher velocity, it is necessary for the banks to equip their customers.
“Two large organizations in France have pledged to systematically distributing bank cards tailored to their customers for contactless use,” said Gerard Nebui, CEO of Visa Europe France.
Cavities are testing payments using a mobile phone.
But this is only the first stage of the development of the future means of payment, i.e. a mobile phone.
So far, only a few devices are equipped with NFC (Harvex communication technology) necessary to make such transactions.
“They were postponed.
"But they have to really scatter next year."
“We are counting on global sales of 80 to 120 million units in 2012,” explains Mark Berten, head of Eurosmart.
But the jars are moving forward.
Yesterday, Crédit Agricole Announced an experiment from December-June next year in Kahna.
Approximately 200 customers and employees will be equipped with covers that provide contactless payments.
BPCE has a similar design in Strasbourg and in Marseille.
And Société Générale also said yesterday that he is preparing to launch an offer into the market, which will allow its customers to pay through their mobile phones.
